This is a known breaking change. Android deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` (API 30+), and .NET MAUI has moved to **edge-to-edge** rendering with **`WindowInsets`-based** keyboard handling in .NET 10.

Here's the modern approach:

### 1. Set `adjustResize` in `AndroidManifest.xml` (still needed)
```xml
<activity
    ...
    android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />
```

### 2. Structure your layout with a `Grid`
```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <!-- Chat messages -->
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}" ... />

    <!-- Input bar — will be pushed up by keyboard -->
    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

### 3. Disable `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` (it conflicts with chat layouts)

In your `Entry` or on the page, turn it off — it tries to scroll individual controls into view, which fights your layout:

```csharp
// In MauiProgram.cs or per-page:
Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect();
```

Or per-control in XAML:
```xml
<Entry
    android:KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Enabled="False"
    xmlns:android="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Platform;assembly=Microsoft.Maui" />
```

### 4. Handle insets explicitly (if needed)

If the above isn't enough, handle the keyboard inset on Android in your platform code:

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new InsetsListener());
}

class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        // imeInsets.Bottom gives you the keyboard height in px
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, imeInsets.Bottom);
        return insets;
    }
}
```

**TL;DR:** Replace the old `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` platform-specific with `adjustResize` in the manifest + proper `Grid` layout + disable `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`. If that's not enough, use `WindowInsetsCompat` to apply bottom padding manually.
